syntax:
For a class with virtual functions:

// Class declaration
class ClassName {
public:
    virtual void func();
    // ... other members
};

// Compiler-generated internal structures
// Each class with virtual functions has a vtable:
// vtable for ClassName: an array of function pointers
// Each object of ClassName contains a hidden pointer to its vtable,
// typically at the beginning of the object layout.

// During object construction, the compiler initializes the vtable pointer:
// object->vptr = &ClassName_vtable;

// Function call via virtual dispatch:
// object->vptr[index](object); // internally, compiler generates code to dereference vptr and call function

code_example:
class Base {
public:
    virtual void f() { /* Base implementation */ }
};
class Derived : public Base {
public:
    void f() override { /* Derived implementation */ }
};

// Memory layout analysis:
// When an object of Derived is instantiated, its memory layout includes:
// [vptr][data members...]
// The vptr points to Derived's vtable, which contains pointers to Derived::f and Base::f.
// During a virtual call, e.g., obj->f(), the compiler generates code to:
// 1. Load the vptr from the object.
// 2. Index into the vtable to find the correct function pointer.
// 3. Call the function via the pointer, passing the object as 'this'.
